---
import FoundationPage from "../components/FoundationPage.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const example = `import {
  createPaginatedRowModel, createSortedRowModel, rowPaginationFeature,
  rowSelectionFeature, rowSortingFeature, tableFeatures, useTable,
} from "@tanstack/react-table";
import { DataTable, DataTablePagination, selectColumn } from "@skryensya/react/data-table";

const features = tableFeatures({
  rowSortingFeature, rowSelectionFeature, rowPaginationFeature,
  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),
  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),
});

/* Defined once, outside the component: TanStack needs stable columns. */
const columns = [
  selectColumn(),
  { accessorKey: "name", header: "Name" },
  { accessorKey: "age", header: "Age" },
];

export function People({ data }) {
  const table = useTable({ features, columns, data });
  return (
    <>
      <DataTable table={table} caption="People" />
      <DataTablePagination table={table} />
    </>
  );
}`;

const features = [
  ["No feature registered", "A plain table: headers, rows and cells, named by its caption."],
  ["Sorting", "Each sortable header becomes a button, and its cell reports aria-sort: none, ascending or descending."],
  ["Row selection", "selectColumn adds a checkbox per row and one in the header for the page. A partly selected page reads as indeterminate."],
  ["Pagination", "DataTablePagination draws numbered pages from the table's own page index."],
];
---

<FoundationPage
  title="TanStack Table"
  description="Use TanStack Table for sorting, selection and paging, and this system's Table for how the data reads."
>
  <p class="lede">
    A headless table owns the state: which column is sorted, which rows are selected, which page is
    showing. It owns no markup. <code>DataTable</code> reads what the table instance already decided and
    says it with this system's Table, so the library stays in charge of the data and the system of how it
    reads.
  </p>

  <h2>One table</h2>
  <p>
    Register only the features the table needs. A feature that is not registered is not drawn, so the
    same <code>DataTable</code> serves a plain table, a sortable one and a selectable one.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={example} label="TSX" />

  <h2>What each feature shows</h2>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th class="sk-table__header">Feature</th>
          <th class="sk-table__header">In the table</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        {
          features.map(([feature, shows]) => (
            <tr class="sk-table__row">
              <td class="sk-table__cell">{feature}</td>
              <td class="sk-table__cell">{shows}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Behaviour to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The instance decides which rows exist. Paging therefore goes through <code>DataTablePagination</code>; the <code>TablePager</code> pattern pages the rows already in the DOM, which is the other model, for a table that renders everything.</li>
    <li>Sorting is announced on the header cell, and the sort button keeps the column's name, so a screen reader says the name and the order together.</li>
    <li>Every checkbox is named. The default names are in English; pass yours to <code>selectColumn</code>.</li>
    <li>Column resizing is this system's: <code>resizableColumns</code> and the other <code>Table</code> options pass through <code>DataTable</code> untouched.</li>
    <li>Define the columns once, outside the component. A new array on every render remounts each header and cell.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Installing</h2>
  <p>
    Import from <code>@skryensya/react/data-table</code>. It needs TanStack Table 9 as an optional peer
    dependency, and loads none of its code itself: only the instance you built runs.
  </p>
</FoundationPage>
